  "narrative": {
    "intro": "Wyoming has recorded $526M in federal contract obligations in FY2026 to date. The state logged 2,114 awards with an average award size of $249K, and obligations are up 5.8% year over year. The Department of the Interior is the largest buyer, obligating $313M, followed by the Department of Defense with $81.8M and the Department of Veterans Affairs with $41.4M. Construction and infrastructure-related industries dominate obligations: Water and Sewer Line and Related Structures Construction leads with $246M, followed by Highway, Street, and Bridge Construction at $62.8M and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction at $57.7M. A small set of vendors capture a large share of dollars: RECORD STEEL AND CONSTRUCTION, INC. received $129M and G.S.E. CONSTRUCTION COMPANY, INC. received $109M. Year-by-year totals show fluctuation in obligations, with FY21 at $390M, FY22 at $559M, FY23 at $470M, FY24 at $532M, FY25 at $497M, and FY26 at $526M. These figures are contract obligations and the current-year totals are partial, fiscal year to date.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "How concentrated is Wyoming federal spending among agencies?",
        "body": "The Department of the Interior is the largest buyer, obligating $313M. The next three buyers are the Department of Defense with $81.8M, the Department of Veterans Affairs with $41.4M, and the Department of Transportation with $39.2M. The top six buyers also include the Department of Agriculture at $28.6M and the General Services Administration at $26.8M. Smaller buyers in the top ten range from $5.6M down to $315K."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Which industries and vendors dominate obligations in Wyoming?",
        "body": "Water and Sewer Line and Related Structures Construction is the largest industry at $246M. Highway, Street, and Bridge Construction is $62.8M and Commercial and Institutional Building Construction is $57.7M. RECORD STEEL AND CONSTRUCTION, INC. and G.S.E. CONSTRUCTION COMPANY, INC. are the largest vendors at $129M and $109M, respectively, followed by THE ARMORED GROUP LLC at $26.4M and AECOM + TETRA TECH JOINT VENTURE at $22.7M."
      }
